## A B S T R A C T
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a novel class of hybrid materials constructed from metals ions/ions clusters and organic linkers. Owing to their well-defined porous structures and other interesting properties, MOFs and their derivatives/composites have shown unprecedented potentials as nanofillers in membranes for various separations. Recently, many encouraging results have been demonstrated in membrane permeability and selectivity when constructing thin-film nanocomposite (TFN) membranes integrated with MOFs in the selective layer for gas and liquid separation. Although impressive progresses have been exhibited, significant challenges remain in lab-scale research. In addition, implementation barriers can be foreseen when commercializing these MOF incorporated TFN membranes. Therefore, we aim to highlight the recent efforts and advances for such challenges and barriers in this review as well as to assess the potential applications of these membranes for molecular separation. The mechanisms for the enhanced separation performance would be analysed, elucidated, and summarized to offer valuable insights for realizing optimal interface morphology and separation perfor­ mance of MOF incorporated TFN membranes.
## 1. Introduction
In recent decades, membrane separation has received unprecedented attention as reliable, safe and affordable processes for separating gas mixtures, treating wastewater, desalinating brackish water or seawater, and reclaiming organic solvents [1–5]. Thin-film composite (TFC) membranes are currently the dominant membrane configuration in various membrane separation processes for gas and liquid separations [6–11]. Typically, they consist of three layers; namely, (1) a porous polymer layer as the gutter layer on top of (2) a non-woven fabric as the mechanical support and (3) an ultrathin dense layer to render the selectivity [12–14]. Although they have already been commercialized for years and are capable of meeting requirements in various separation tasks, researchers are still devoted to further advancing their separation performance, lowering energy consumption and footprint, and widening the applications. One of research foci is to have breakthroughs on membrane materials to surpass the inherent “trade-off” relationship between permeability and selectivity.
Kulprathipanja et al. in 1988 were the pioneers inventing mixed matrix membranes (MMMs) consisting of zeolite nanoparticles and polymers exceeding the upper bounds between selectivity and perme­ ability for conventional polymers [15]. Early MMMs mainly focused on gas separation [16,17]. Later, Jeong et al. in Prof. Hoek’s group, for the first time, embedded zeolites in the polyamide selective layer of RO membranes with impressive permeability and selectivity for seawater desalination [18]. Since then, a diverse set of engineered nanomaterials (both nonporous and porous) have been explored for both liquid and gas separations [14,19–25]. Among them, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of porous crystals made of inorganic metals ions/ ions clusters and organic bridging ligands in regular and geometric patterns [26]. Since their first discovery in 1990 by Hoskins and Robson [27], research focuses on MOFs have gradually shifted from the syn­ theses and fundamentals to utilization. The vast species of metals ions/ ions clusters and organic ligands, together with tremendous possibilities in their combinations, allow for extremely variant structures (i.e., size, shape, pore architecture, etc.) and versatile functions. Combined with facile modifications, MOFs have attracted intense interest and shown great potentials in diverse applications, such as catalysis, gas storage and drug delivery [28–34].
In the field of membrane separation, MOFs and their engineered forms have also been poised and intensively studied to solve the tradeoff relationship between permeability and selectivity [28,35–37]. First, their well-defined nano-size channels are capable of repelling solutes with sizes greater than the channel openings but allowing the smaller molecules to transport through. By manipulating the composi­ tion, the pore-window of MOFs can range from $0.2\mathrm{nm}$ to $11\mathrm{nm}$ , rending them molecular sieving functions in the regimes of gas separation, reverse osmosis, nanofiltration, forward osmosis, etc. [38,39]. At the same time, due to the crystalline nature of MOFs, the pore structure of MOFs is relatively rigid and solid, so that they are able to realize effective molecular sieving without suffering from the trade-off rela­ tionship which plagues conventional polymer membranes [40,41]. In addition, MOFs possess a higher porosity than dense polymer mem­ branes, allowing more rapid permeation of solutes/solvents [42]. Although polymeric microporous materials like polymers of intrinsic microporosity (PIMs) and covalent-organic frameworks (COFs) possess better compatibility and processability due to their polymeric nature, they suffer from accelerated aging and loss of porosity. While inorganic microporous species like zeolites lack structure diversity, chemical tunability, and processability [35]. Thus, combining features of rela­ tively stable porosity, high tunability and processability, MOFs have attracted intensive research to transform lab-synthesized MOF powders to commercialized membrane products.
Despite pure MOF thin films may surpass the upper bound of tradi­ tional TFC membranes, the fabrication strategy towards defect-free, flexible and large-scale pure MOF films still remains as a huge and imperative challenge [28,43–46]. While compared to MMMs, a little amount of MOF dosage in the ultrathin selective layer is sufficient to alter the properties and separation performance of thin-film nano­ composite (TFN) membranes [35]. Therefore, at the current stage, MOF incorporated TFN membranes, combining both merits of MOFs and TFC membranes (Fig. 1a) and requiring minimal dosage, are much more attractive and processable than pure MOF films and MMMs. It also should be noted that while the inherently high permselectivity of MOFs stimulated intensive research on their applications in TFN membranes in the first place, researchers continuously discovered additional func­ tionalities of some MOFs highly desirable for membrane separation, such as hydrophilicity, adsorption, and antifouling capability. The number of related publications increases steadily as shown in Fig. 1b.
However, most researchers have encountered technical obstacles during fabrication of MOF TFN membranes, such as non-selective permeation pathways formed surrounding the MOF nanoparticles in the selective layer owing to the unsatisfactory compatibility between MOFs and polymeric materials. Many recent efforts have been devoted to identi­ fying, theoretically studying, and preventing the formation of these nonselective channelings. There is an urgent need for a summary and deeper understanding of these materials and their effects on membrane struc­ tures with aims to achieve supreme separation performance ultimately. In this review, we would focus on their current research status, espe­ cially engineering of MOFs, manipulation of interfacial properties, as well as efforts towards better understanding on underlying mechanisms for performance enhancements.
## 2. Fabrication of MOFs and MOF TFN membranes
## 2.1. MOFs and their derivatives/composites
## 2.1.1. General background of MOFs
Due to more recent emergence of MOFs compared to other porous materials such as zeolites, the development of MOF-based TFN mem­ branes are still at the nascent stage [47]. Especially, the applications of some early MOFs in membrane-based liquid separation were largely constrained by their insufficient stability as even ambient moisture could lead to their degradation [48,49]. After extensive research efforts, this obstacle has been curdled by developing new types of MOFs with relatively high stability in water. Among them, metal carboxylate frameworks containing high-valence metal ions (e.g. MIL and UiO se­ ries) [50–55], metal azolate frameworks consisting of nitrogen-donor ligands like zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF) series [56–61], and their derivatives/composites have been exploited as nanofillers in TFN membranes (Fig. 1c) [35,49].
In most previous works, the MOFs incorporated in TFC membranes possessed a three-dimensional (3D) structure. While more recently, water-stable two-dimensional (2D) MOFs made from the coordination between divalent cations and hexaaminobenzene or benzoquinoid with a graphene-like layered structure gradually stand out for membrane separation (Fig. 1d) [62–68]. In particular, after the exfoliation process, 2D MOFs exhibit an ultrathin thickness with abundant functional groups, allowing them with lower mass transfer resistance and better compatibility in the polymeric matrix. Prof. Zhang was the pioneer studying TFN membranes incorporated with Cu-tetrahydroxy-1,4- quinone (Cu-THQ) 2D MOF nanoparticles (Fig. 2a) [64]. The hydro­ philic Cu-THQ had hexagonal pores about $1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ highly suitable for permeation of water or solvent. The resultant membrane not only had a pure water permeance of 2.4 times higher than the control but also had a slightly enhanced salt rejection. In another work, ultrasonically exfoli­ ated nickel-based 2D MOFs were embedded in TFN RO membranes via $m$ -phenylenediamine (MPD) aqueous solution during the interfacial polymerization [65]. The TFN membrane consisting of $0.015\mathrm{wt\%}$ of this 2D MOFs possessed a pure water permeance of 2.5 times higher than the pristine thin TFC membranes while maintaining the NaCl rejection of $99.2~\%$ .
## 2.1.2. MOF derivatives
The surface of MOFs can also be functionalized through a judicious choice of organic ligands to obtain specific favourable functionalities or better compatibility in the polymeric matrix [54,69–74]. Higher load­ ings of MOFs often result in the formation of peripheral macrovoids and a defective selective layer due to the partial inorganic nature and ag­ gregation of MOFs [14,24]. In addition, owing to their nano-sizes, the leaching of MOFs from TFN membranes into the retentate and permeate streams is inevitable if there are no strong interactions between MOFs and polymeric matrices. This may lead to poor sustainability of mem­ brane performance and harmful implications in environmental and health. However, these concerns have been minimized due to the highly chemical diversity of MOFs and the advancements in post-synthetic modification methods. Generally, by tailoring the surface chemistry of MOFs, literatures have shown that one can (1) promote the dispersion of MOFs in the aqueous or organic phases, (2) improve their integration with polymer matrices, and (3) avoid leaching of MOFs from the membranes.
For example, the $\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ groups on $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can react with tri­ mesoyl chloride (TMC) to form covalent bonding with the polyamide matrix. Xiao et al. compared the desalination performance of TFN membranes consisting of UiO-66 and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanoparticles with a similar size of $\sim50~\mathrm{{nm}}$ [75]. Results indicated that TFN membranes with a less amount $(0.1\mathrm{wt/v\%})$ of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exhibited a much higher water permeance than those with $0.2\mathrm{wt}/\mathrm{v}\%$ UiO-66 nanoparticles while retaining a comparable rejection to $\mathrm{Na_{2}S O_{4}}$ . In addition, $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ has been furthered functionalized by small organics to avoid the for­ mation of non-selective voids around MOF nanoparticles, such as tannic acid [76,77], polydopamine (PDA) [78–80], palmitoyl chloride [81], dodecyl aldehyde [82], and imidazole-2-carbaldehyde [83].
Dai et al. modified MIL-101(Cr) nanoparticles with ethylenediamine which could be grafted to the coordinately unsaturated metal sites of MIL-101(Cr) [84]. The modified MOFs comprised both negatively charged carboxyl groups on the surface and positively charged amine groups in the interior channels. They showed remarkable capabilities to repel pharmaceuticals with different charges. Another group modified MIL-101 (Cr) by grafting tetraethylenepentamine (TEPA) and then blended them into poly (ether-block amide) (Pebax $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ 1657) [7]. The resultant TFN membrane displayed a large increase in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeability, which was $63\ \%$ higher than the plain Pebax membrane. Besides, the former had a $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity of $110\%$ greater than the latter.
Functionalization of MOFs has also been attempted to tune their pore structure. Bonnet et al. manipulated the channel dimension and pore size distribution of pocket-channel (PCN) frameworks; namely, PCN-222 nanorods with a large pore of $37\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ , through binding of myristic acid [85]. In contrast, Liao et al. [86] and Chen et al. [87] etched ZIF-8 nanoparticles by tannic acid to form hydrophilic hollow structures favourable for water or solvent transport (Fig. 2b). ZIF-8 and UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ have also been induced with nano-sized defects (shown in Fig. 2c as white dots) by adopting a defect engineering methodology for TFN RO membranes [53,61].
## 2.1.3. MOF composites/hybrids
MOF composites/hybrids, mostly integrated with carbon-based nanomaterials, like graphene oxide (GO) [88–90], carbon nanotubes (CNTs) [91], carbon nanofibers (CNFs) [92] and carbon dots (CDs) [93], have been proposed not only to solve the aggregation and instability problems of MOFs in the polymeric matrix but also to manipulate the membrane structure or render the MOFs with multi-functionality such as antifouling propensity. He et al. uniformly distributed UiO-66 nano­ particles on sulfonated GO by an in-situ growth method (Fig. 2d) [88] and then incorporated the modified nanomaterial into TFN FO mem­ branes with improved stability and performance for the removal of heavy metals. Firouzjaei et al. also uniformly dispersed Ag-MOF nano­ particles on the surface of GO nanosheets using an ultrasonic irradiation method and then embedded them into the selective layer of FO mem­ branes for supreme antimicrobial properties [90]. Prof. Lee’s team prepared hybrids of CNT and ZIF-8 (Fig. 2e), and exploited the advan­ tages of both 2D and 3D porous nanomaterials in TFN membranes for seawater desalination [91]. Wang et al. in-situ loaded ultrafine CDs into the intrinsic apertures of ZIF-8 with the aim of mitigating their leaching from the selective layer and maintaining their high chlorine-tolerant capability [93].
## 2.2. Incorporation methods
The approach to incorporate MOFs into TFN membranes plays a decisive role in their locations and distribution in the selective layer, thus affecting the configuration, topology and physiochemical proper­ ties as well as separation performance of the resultant membranes [94]. Generally, interfacial polymerization formed TFN membranes prepared by pre-mixing MOFs in the organic phase tend to position MOFs in the upper layer of the polyamide matrix, while dispersing MOFs in the aqueous phase tends to locate them in the bottom layer. A common challenge existing among the current approaches is to prevent the agglomeration of MOFs within the polymeric matrix. In addition, to reduce the material cost because of the relatively expensive MOFs, some researches aimed to use minimal MOFs while to achieve the optimal performance. Although it could reduce the amount of MOFs used in TFN membranes, one must consider if this cost-cutting approach can be retrofitted into the existing manufacturing processes for TFC membranes as well as the cost-effective issue of producing such membranes.
To save the amount of MOFs during the fabrication of TFN mem­ branes via interfacial polymerization, MOF nanoparticles or interlayers can be deposited onto the substrates prior to interfacial polymerization by alternative protocols rather than the conventional method [83,85,95,96], for instance, in-situ growth/assembly [57,97–100], spray-coating [64,76,101,102], spin-coating [103], filtration [69,104,105,106], and co-deposition [81]. An innovative approach to position a monolayer of MIL-101 (Cr) in TFN membranes was demon­ strated in 2018 where a Langmuir-Schaefer film of MOFs was placed between the thin polyamide layer and the P84 substrate (Fig. 3a, the middle image). Up to then, this method required the least amount of MOFs (i.e., $3.8\upmu\mathrm{gcm}^{-2})$ [107]. Spin-coating can be employed to deposit a uniform thin film onto flat substrates by the means of centrifugal forces. Usually, a solution containing MOFs is loaded onto the center of a substrate and a controlled high-speed centrifugal force spreads the so­ lution as a thin film. After solvent evaporation, a MOF layer is formed on the substrate. The film thickness and other properties can be adjusted by the spin speed, solvent evaporation rate, and viscosity of the coating solution. Liu et al. spin-coated a thin film consisting of poly­ dimethylsiloxane (PDMS) and MOF as a gutter layer to enhance the gas transport for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture [106]. After interfacial polymerization, the MOFs could be placed at the bottom of the selective layer as a contin­ uous or a discrete interlayer between the substrate and the polyamide layer, as depicted in Fig. 3a.
As aforementioned, MOFs can be also introduced into TFN mem­ branes via the organic monomer solutions during interfacial polymerization [108–112]. However, the agglomeration of MOF nano­ particles in the oil phase is a key challenge especially at a relatively higher loading. The agglomerated MOFs tend to locate in the selective layer and possibly generate deformities within the membrane structure. Thus, surface engineering of MOFs to promote their dispersity in the oil phase must be advanced to minimize the defects in the selective layer. In addition, sedimentation of MOFs must be avoided during the production of TFN membranes to reduce their agglomeration.
Besides the conventional interfacial polymerization, there are other approaches to fabricate TFN membranes consisting of MOFs, especially for gas separation, like spin-coating [113], dip-coating [114,115], and solution casting methods [116]. Dip-coating is another effective and easy method to directly incorporate MOFs into TFC membranes for gas separation. Usually, the substrate is immersed into a solution tank containing MOFs and a thin layer of MOFs is formed onto the substrate when being pulled up from the tank and dried. Mousavinejad et al. fabricated such TFN membranes for $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ separation by dip-coating a polyethersulfone (PES) support in a homogeneous Pebax/Ni- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC solution for $5\mathrm{min}$ [115].
In Table 1, we have summarized and incorporation methods as well as discussion of their respective features and feasibility for commercial production. Although the above methods may work in lab-scale research, one must consider their feasibility to be implemented in the current procedures of manufacturing TFC membranes in order to ach­ ieve a continuous, scalable and cost-effective process. For instance, some pre-loading methods, like in-situ growth and vacuum filtration, may face great difficulties in terms of scaling-up, process continuity, and stability of MOFs atop the substrates before interfacial polymerization. In this regard, dip-coating, spray-coating or evaporation can be more readily integrated into the current production lines for TFC membranes, while their commercial viability still needs further assessment in many aspects. For cases of mixing MOFs in the monomer solutions for inter­ facial polymerization, although it is most facile to be implemented into the current manufacturing process for TFC membranes, one must (1) minimize the MOF agglomeration in the solutions to ensure membrane integrity and (2) have a stable and accurate MOF concentration in the solutions for interfacial polymerization.
## 3. Impacts of MOFs on separation and other performances
Separation performance in terms of permeability and selectivity is the key indicator to evaluate the effectiveness and efficiency of mem­ brane separation processes, whereas the current TFC membranes are subjected to a ubiquitous and pernicious trade-off relationship between permeability and selectivity. MOFs, with characteristics of rigid and engineerable pore structure and superior molecular sieving capability, stand out in pursuit of surpassing the upper bounds. Together with some other factors, such as enhanced hydrophilicity or hydrophobicity, improved surface charge properties of selective layers, and modified cross-linking degrees and mechanisms, MOF incorporated TFN mem­ branes are in a favourable position to outperform the bare TFC mem­ branes with simultaneously higher permeability and selectivity. Their separation performance may also sustain better in the continuous longterm operation, benefiting from the rigid pore structure of MOFs [117]. Possible mechanisms responsible for the changes in separation perfor­ mance have also been probed by analysing morphologies and physi­ ochemical properties of membranes. Besides separation performance, some other features of MOF TFN membranes, like antifouling, boron rejection and chlorine resistance, have also been investigated and will be elaborated below.
## 3.1. Membrane permeability
MOF incorporated TFN membranes usually exhibit a higher perme­ ability as expected while the increment rate may vary according to the inherent property, particle size, loading and position of MOFs [58,94]. The underneath mechanisms for the enhanced permeability are quite complicated since membrane permeability is not only subjected to the property changes (e.g. hydrophilicity, polarity, and charge) in the polymeric matrix but also the membrane topology (i.e., surface rough­ ness). To elaborate it, we would firstly discuss the effects of different MOF positions on membrane permeability in the selective layer.
Fig. 3a shows the first situation where the MOFs are pre-loaded on the substrates before interfacial polymerization. The improvement in membrane permeability might be hard to be interpreted because the additional MOF layer also increases the overall transport resistance. Since the molecules travelled through the polyamide layer may be blocked by the non-porous part of the substrate underneath, the pene­ trants need to travel longer paths across the selective layer. A porous MOF interlayer (often referred to as a gutter layer) cannot only lower the tortuosity but also shorten the transport paths to surpass the adverse effects from the additional transport resistance. This phenomenon is called as the “gutter or gutter layer” effect [94,101].
In addition, this MOF interlayer may participate with the interfacial polymerization and tailor the cross-linking degree via 1) reacting with the part of monomers, 2) influencing the uptake and release of amine monomers, 3) changing the interface for the interfacial polymerization reaction, and etc. Zhao et al. found that although no $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanoparticle existed on the membrane surface, the composition of the selective layer has been changed according to the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis with a depth of analysis about $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The degree of cross-linking was reduced substantially from 0.7 to 0.2–0.4 because some acyl chloride groups of TMC have already reacted with the amino groups of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ [101]. This much lower degree of crosslinking resulted in a looser and more permeable selective layer due to the effect of the underneath MOF layer. Interestingly, Wen et al. found that the degree of cross-linking was increased from 0.74 to 0.93 due to the existence of the 2D MOF nanosheet interlayer [68]. The discrepancy in the trend of cross-linking degree between Zhao et al. [91] and Wen et al. [58] might result from different MOF interlayer morphologies since the 3D-MOF interlayer was discrete while the 2D MOF interlayer was more continuous. As a result, they have differences in uptake and release of amine monomer during the interfacial polymerization.
If MOF nanoparticles are dispersed on the top surface (Fig. 3b) [118], they can directly tailor the properties of membrane surfaces such as increasing surface hydrophilicity with a higher affinity towards water molecules. Together with their impacts on interfacial polymerization as discussed above, the membrane permeability can be remarkably improved. For the third scenario as depicted in Fig. 3c, the nano-sized channels in MOFs can facilitate the water transport unimpededly. In addition, similar to other hydrophilic nanomaterials like Ag nano­ particles, nano-sized peripheral channels around MOFs might be formed during interfacial polymerization (Fig. 3c). These microchannels arise from the termination of interfacial polymerization around hydrophilic MOFs due to the hydrolysis of TMC monomers by water stored in MOFs’ inner structures [119,120]. Guo et al. have observed these cavities by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) in the polymeric matrix sur­ rounding their star-shaped ZIF-8 nanoparticles [56]. The existence of peripheral channels around MOFs can further increase the porosity in the selective layer and facilitate the water transport.
Recently, researchers have also incorporated MOFs as sacrificial nanofillers to create nano-voids (Fig. 3d) [121,122]. For example, nonhydrostable MOFs (ZIF-67) were integrated into the polyamide layers and then decomposed in the presence of water to generate nano-sized voids in the selective layer for a higher water permeability in RO [121]. Similarly, ZIF-67 MOFs were adopted in a facile MOF selfsacrifice strategy to design nanofiltration (NF) membranes with a highly permselective layer [122]. The resultant membranes had a rejection of $99.2~\%$ to the Congo Red dye $(500~\mathrm{ppm})$ and a water per­ meance of $12.6\mathrm{L}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ (LMH/bar).
## 3.2. Membrane selectivity
For liquid separation, the change in solute rejection after MOF incorporation is dependent on many factors. Besides the size exclusion effects from MOFs, the solute-MOF interactions such as the suppressed hydrophobic interactions between hydrophilic MOFs and hydrophobic solutes play an important role on rejection [38]. In addition, negatively charged MOFs on membrane surface can endow electrostatic repulsion to anions in the feed solution [41]. Gupta et al. in Prof. Jiang group molecularly simulated the rejection of NaCl by various pure ZIF films consisting of different functional groups. All films had rejections varying from ${\sim}97~\%$ to $100\%$ . They concluded that in addition to the aperture size, the polarity of functional groups was a critical parameter [70]. However, non-selective voids or defects formed in the selective layer of TFN membranes due to MOF aggregation and poor compatibility with the polymeric matrix undeniably led to various degrees of membrane deterioration especially at a high MOF loading [14,24]. Consequently, most MOF incorporated TFN membranes experimentally exhibited little improvement or even jeopardy in solute rejection. This has stimulated the recent studies focusing on the surface modification of MOFs in TFN membranes to optimize the interface morphology between MOFs and polymeric matrices [76–83].
For gas separation, the unique functional groups on MOFs may endow the membranes with a higher selectivity towards a specific gas component. For example, $-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ groups show high affinity towards polar $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . Therefore, compared with the pristine membranes, the TFN membranes modified by $1.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF- ${\mathsf{8-N H}}_{2}$ increased the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ perme­ ability by $228\%$ and the selectivity of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ over $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ by $106\%$ at $0.2\mathrm{MPa}$ (Fig. 4a) [123]. Overall, the separation performance of ZIF- ${\cdot}8{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ modified TFN membranes for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CO_{2}/N O}$ and ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}/{\mathrm{He}}$ separations all surpassed the Robeson’s upper bounds. Metal-ion coordination derived cross-linking has also proved to be an effective method for the enhanced gas selectivity after MOF incorporation. Zhu et al. found that the coordination bonds were constructed between ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and carboxyl groups in polyimide-COOH/UiO- ${\cdot}66/{\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ TFN membranes when immersing the TFN membranes into a $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ solution (Fig. 4b). The modified TFN membranes not only had a higher $\mathrm{CO_{2}}/\mathrm{CH_{4}}$ selectivity (i. e., 29–35 vs 7.5–17.4) but also a higher plasticization pressure (i.e., 0.3–0.9 vs 0.9–1.5 MPa) than the unmodified ones [116].
## 3.3. Antifouling propensity
Enhancements in antifouling properties of TFC membranes are of great interest in real applications as severe fouling will substantially deteriorate the filtration efficiency and increase the maintenance and replenishment costs [124–127]. This can be achieved by manipulating the properties and morphology of membrane surface and also intro­ duction of antifouling/biocidal nanomaterials into the selective layers. Aljundi incorporated $0.4\mathrm{\wt\%}$ of ZIF-8 in the polyamide layer and remarkably promoted the surface hydrophilicity (i.e., water contact angle dropped from $62.8^{\circ}$ to $33.2^{\circ}.$ ). The resultant membranes not only had an enhanced permeance but also remarkably reduced the fouling of bovine serum albumin (BSA) by $>75\ \%$ due to the weakened hydro­ phobic interaction between the membrane surface and organic foulants [108].
Considering the shortcomings of pure Ag nanoparticles as antibac­ terial agents (e.g. expensive and need to be replenished due to the gradual dissolution), Ag-based MOFs (Ag-MOFs) and their composites have been developed and introduced into the selective layers for the same purpose [90,96,111,128]. Firouzjaei et al. incorporated a com­ posite consisting of GO and silver-based MOFs (Ag-MOFs) into the TFC selective layer, the resultant TFN membranes showed notably improvements on anti-biofouling properties and less affinity towards organic foulants (i.e., sodium alginate) [90]. The TFN membranes doped with GO/Ag-MOFs inhibited approximately $96~\%~E.$ coli growth, which was much higher than the pristine TFC $(2\%)$ , GO TFN $(66~\%)$ , and AgMOF TFN membranes $(80~\%)$ . Their greater antimicrobial activity resulted from the synergetic effects of GO and Ag-MOFs, which together led to membrane surfaces with more negative charges, greater hydro­ philicity, lower surface roughness, and higher toxicity to E. coli.
In addition, the photocatalytic properties of some MOF materials can render the membranes with self-cleaning ability after being fouled. For example, Zn-TCPP laminates, one type of porphyrin based 2D MOFs, exhibit excellent photocatalytic properties. By loading such MOFs, their corresponding TFN membranes could degrade the dyes attached on the membrane surface via photocatalysis under visible light and thus fully recover the water flux to the original state after fouling (Fig. 5) [105].
## 3.4. Boron rejection and chlorine resistance
Traditional TFC membranes, particularly for RO processes, still suffer from inadequate rejections to small neutral solutes like boron in seawater which may cause nausea, dizziness, kidney failure and other health problems for humans [129]. By blending $0.05\mathrm{wt\%}$ of UiO-66, Liu et al. improved the boron rejection of RO membranes from $82.3~\%$ to $91.2\ \%$ , resulting from the chemisorption of boric acid by UiO-66 nanoparticles which had a greater adsorption capacity than many con­ ventional adsorbents [118]. Additionally, owing to the partial blockage of internal nanochannels in UiO-66 by the adsorbed boron, subsequent boron diffusion across the UiO-66 was also hindered.
Another well acknowledged challenge of polyamide TFC membranes is their low tolerance to oxidants since the amide bonds are not resilient under a prolonged exposure to oxidants like free chlorine and NaClO, which however, are commonly utilized in membrane washing or for the control of biofouling [130,131]. Cu-THQ 2D MOFs were found by Prof. Zhang’s group to significantly improve the chlorine resistance of the TFN membranes, partially due to the formation of ester bonds between Cu-THQ and TMC which was more stable than amide bonds when being exposed to oxidants [64]. Similarly, Zhao et al. discovered that the UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ loaded TFN membranes showed less susceptibility to chlorine [101]. The enhanced chlorine resistance could be attributed to two factors: 1) active chlorine was more likely to attack the amide bonds between UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanoparticles and TMC or the free amino groups on UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , instead of the amide bonds between MPD and TMC, and 2) UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ imparted additional blockage to the solute passages even after the selective layer was impaired by chlorine [101,130]. In another work, Wang et al. reported that the $\mathrm{CD}s@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ nanocomposites also protected the polyamide layers from chlorine attack since (1) the hydrogen bonding between CDs and polyamide matrix hindered the substitution of imine hydrogen with chlorine, and (2) the active site of MPD residual might be protected by the inherently electron-rich CDs [93].
## 4. Applications of MOF TFN membranes
Owing to the tuneable properties of MOFs and their flexible combi­ nation with the selective layer, MOF TFN membranes can have adjust­ able interior architectures and therefore wide applications for molecular separation. Table 2 and Table 3 tabulate the MOFs used, loading methods, and optimized separation performances of most recent MOF TFN membranes in literatures for liquid and gas separations, respec­ tively. In this part, we particularly focused on works which had features of rational design, outstanding separation performance, in-depth struc­ ture/mechanism studies or long-term performance.
## 4.1. Reverse osmosis (RO)
RO has been intensively applied to desalinate brackish water or seawater, as well as to perform as the last step in advanced wastewater treatment and reclamation processes [129]. Currently, TFC membranes are no doubt the state-of-the-art type for industrial RO processes. MOFs with suitable pore sizes, like ZIF-8 $(0.34~\mathrm{\nm})$ and UiO-66 series $(0.42\mathrm{-}0.60\mathrm{nm})$ can function and show screening effects to the hydrated $\mathsf{V a}^{+}\ (0.72\ \mathrm{nm})$ and $\mathrm{cl^{-}}$ $\left(0.66~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ ions while allowing the transport of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\left(0.28\mathrm{nm}\right)$ . To target the complete removal of all solutes from water, it requires the RO membranes to have a stringent standard in both structure integrity and membrane selectivity. Up to date, using pure MOF films to successfully achieve this goal has rarely been reported. Liu et al. fabricated a UiO-66 thin film with a thickness of around $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ on an alumina hollow fiber and deployed this membrane for desalination [132]. Although the aperture diameter of UiO-66 $\left(0.60\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ was theo­ retically small enough to retain hydrated $\mathrm{Na}^{+}(0.72\mathrm{nm})$ , $\mathrm{K}^{+}\left(0.66~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ and $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ $\cdot0.66\ \mathrm{nm})$ ions, the rejections to hydrated monovalent ions $\mathrm{{(Na^{+}}}$ : $47.0~\%$ , $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ : $45.7~\%$ were still not high enough for desalination probably due to the formation of defects within the film. Thus, the molecular sieving function of MOFs for RO has been studied mainly in the form of TFN membranes.
Among 3D MOFs, hydrostable ZIF-8, built with tetrahedral metal ions and imidazole-based organic linkers, have drawn intensive atten­ tion as nanofillers in TFN RO membranes [61,102,108,133]. Duan et al. developed ZIF-8 doped TFN RO membranes and obtained an increment in water permeance to 3.35 LMH/bar, which was $162\%$ higher than the bare polyamide layer, while the NaCl rejection was also substantially improved from 98.1 to $99.5\%$ [133]. They attributed this improvement in water permeance to a faster water transport in more hydrophobic pores of ZIF-8 which had a lower affinity toward water molecules. The effects of MOF particle size on the separation performance of TFN RO membranes have also been investigated based on ZIF-8 [102]. Lee et al. prepared three different sized (60, 150, and $250\mathrm{nm}$ ) ZIF-8 nanoparticles and incorporated them by pre-loading on the substrate and formed a “gutter layer” between the substrate and polyamide matrix. The size of particles would strongly influence the deposition amount and the interface area between MOFs and polyamide layer according to the classic particle deposition theory (Fig. 6a). Under the influence of these two factors at the same time, TFN membranes with $160\mathrm{nm}$ sized ZIF-8 produced the highest water permeance (3.95 LMH/bar) and NaCl rejection $(99.2~\%)$ ).
In another work, Zhao et al. synthesized a series of defective ZIF-8 nanoparticles with a tuneable degree of inner defects by a doublesolvent mediated overgrowth methodology [61]. With a loading of
$0.15~\mathrm{wt\%}$ defective ZIF-8 in the TMC solution, the resultant TFN mem­ branes showed a water permeance of 2.61 LMH/bar and a salt rejection of $98.6~\%$ for desalination of brackish water at 20 bar. This water per­ meance was $52\%$ higher than the control TFC membranes because the defective ZIF-8 provided additional transport paths for water molecules.
Regarding the mechanism study, the superior performance of TFN RO membranes with an interlayer of 3D or 2D MOFs is ascribed to the well-established “gutter layer” effect [65,68,76,101,134]. In a Prof Tang’s study, 2D ZnTCPP interlayered TFN membranes exhibited remarkable improvements in both membrane permeance (from 1.71 to
4.82 LMH/bar) and selectivity (from 95.6 to $97.4~\%$ simultaneously [68]. As illustrated in Fig. 6b, except the “gutter layer” effect during the filtration process, the 2D MOF interlayer also promoted the membrane permeance through altering the structure and morphology of the se­ lective layer in two aspects: (1) the dense MOF layer prevented the escape of nanobubbles formed during the interfacial polymerization, yielding a ridge-and-valley rather than a nodular surface architecture which gave a larger surface area for filtration, and (2) the diffusivity of MPD monomers during interfacial polymerization was retarded by this MOF interlayer which reduced the actual thickness of the selective layer [68].
## 4.2. Nanofiltration (NF)
NF membranes are usually applied to remove multivalent ions and inorganic/organic solutes of $<5$ nm from water. Different types of target components for diverse applications have stimulated vigorous re­ searches on MOFs based NF membranes, including mixed matrix membranes [135,136], pure MOF films on polymeric substrates [60,137,138], and TFN membranes [139]. Benefiting from less MOF usage, the MOF incorporated TFN NF membranes have been intensively investigated for desalination [41,75,83,86,95,96,140,141] and removal of dyes [50,73,122,142,143], heavy metals [144], antibiotics/pharma­ ceuticals [84,117,143] and other small compounds [109,145].
Depending on solute sizes, one can choose MOFs which have rela­ tively bigger screening pore-windows and embed them into NF mem­ branes. Dai et al. fabricated TFN NF membranes doped with MIL-101 (Cr) (pentagonal: $1.2~\mathrm{\nm}$ , hexagonal: $1.6~\mathrm{\nm})$ for rejection of endocrine-disrupting compounds [145]. The water flux of the optimal TFN membranes was 39.5 LMH which was 1.3 times higher than the TFC membranes (17.5 LMH). The rejections of methylparaben, propylpar­ aben, benzylparaben, and bisphenol A were 47.4, 45.9, 51.1, and 79.8 $\%$ , respectively. They were substantially higher than the respective values of the control (27.7, 25.2, 31.3, and $64.9~\%$ , respectively). In a follow-up work, they developed dually charged MOFs by grafting eth­ ylenediamine onto MIL-101(Cr) and dosed them in the selective layer for removal of pharmaceuticals in wastewater [84]. Their membranes showed a higher water permeance (from 17.3 to 24.6 LMH/bar) and remarkably improved rejections to positively charged pharmaceutical active compounds while retaining high rejections to negatively charged ones.
Liu et al. found that MOF TFN membranes could possess higher sustainability under hydraulic pressures because the pure water permeability (PWP) of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ TFN membranes only dropped $2.7\%$ in a 72-h continuous NF process [141]. Cheng et al. drew the same conclusion after they monitored the water permeances of TFC and UiO66 (Ti) incorporated TFN membranes in a continuous NF process for $100\mathrm{~h~}$ at 10 bar [117]. The permeance decline of TFN membranes was evidently less pronounced than that of the control (i.e., 4 vs $14\%$ . This discrepancy arose from the solid nature of MOFs and also probable limited chain movement of polyamide in the presence of MOFs, while the pure polymeric matrix underwent compaction as pores collapsed inevitably under hydraulic pressures.
Xu et al. in Prof. Xu’s group showed that the selectivity towards different monovalent cations could be significantly enhanced by immobilizing Ti exchanged $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in the selective layer of TFN membranes, which was highly desirable for the extraction of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ from seawater [146]. The pore size of UiO-66 (Ti)– ${\mathrm{.NH}}_{2}$ was $0.80\ \mathrm{nm}$ which enabled the permeation of hydrated $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ ( $\mathrm{.0.72\nm}\mathrm{,}$ and ${\mathbf{}}{\mathbf{}}{\mathbf{}}^{+}$ $(0.76~\mathrm{nm})$ ions while screening out hydrated $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ ions $(0.86~\mathrm{nm})$ , rendering the TFN membranes with higher permselectivities of $\mathrm{P_{Na+/Mg2+}}=13.44$ and $\mathbf{P_{\mathrm{Li+/Mg2+}}}=11.38$ than those of the bare TFC membranes $(\mathrm{P_{Na+/Mg2+}}=$ 3.54 and $\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{Li+/Mg2+}}=2.23\$ .
## 4.3. Organic solvent nanofiltration (OSN)
OSN is an emerging application of membranes for molecular sepa­ ration and purification of organic solvents used in petrochemistry, biotechnology and pharmaceutical industries [147–150]. Currently, both TFC and TFN membranes have been explored for OSN. However, both the substrate and selective layers must have adequate resistance to harsh organic solvents. By embedding solvent-resistant nanofillers like MOFs into the selective layer, one may design TFN membranes with better separation performance and sustainability in OSN operations [57,59,151–154].
Sorribas et al. in Prof Livingston’s group first fabricated and studied MOF incorporated TFN membranes for OSN [110]. They employed four different kinds of MOFs; namely, ZIF-8, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al), MIL-53(Al), and MIL-101(Cr), and compared their OSN performance. Based on BET analyses, the pore size and porosity of MOFs increased according to the sequence of $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}53(\mathrm{Al})<\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}53(\mathrm{Al})<\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8<\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}).$ Interestingly, the methanol permeances of these TFN membranes also followed the same order, indicating a close relationship between MOFs’ pore structure and solvent transport. Prof. Shao’s group systematically studied the effects of functionalized ligands in MOFs and post-synthetic functionalization by comparing the TFN membranes embedded with UiO-66, UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , UiO-66- $(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}.$ , and UiO-66-(Ti) [117]. The pore apertures of the first three UiO particles followed the order of UiO-66 $(0.60\ \mathrm{nm})>\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(0.51\ \mathrm{nm})>\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}$ $(0.42\ \mathrm{nm})$ . As a result, the TFN membranes comprising UiO-66 had the highest in­ crements in water/solvent permeances (i.e., ${\sim}84\%$ for water), while the rejections to dyes were well maintained. In addition, defects generated by the post-synthetic exchange between $z\mathbf{r}$ and Ti ions in UiO-66, yiel­ ded MOFs with the highest porosity. Thus, the resultant TFN membrane had the highest water/solvent permeances. These two works have well elucidated the prominent role of the additional passageways provided by MOFs to design TFN membranes for OSN with a faster solvent transport.
2D MOFs have also been investigated in TFN OSN membranes. CuTHQ TFN membranes prepared by Li et al. showed an impressive N, $N_{\mathbf{\delta}}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) permeance of 16.9 LMH/bar and $97.4~\%$ rejection to Allura Red, while in contrast, the neat TFC membranes only exhibited 12.2 LMH/bar and $89.9~\%$ , respectively [64]. Navarro et al. demonstrated that the presence of a MIL-101 (Cr) monolayer, assembled by a bottom-up Langmuir-Schaefer technique, helped achieve a high methanol permeance of 10.1 LMH/bar without significant decreases in membrane rejection to dyes, like sunset yellow $(90.2~\%$ , $450\ \mathrm{Da})$ and rose bengal $(98.0~\%$ , 1017 Da) [107]. The mechanisms of the above improvements in solvent filtration have been discussed in Section 3.1. Recently, Chen et al. also incorporated hollow MOFs (i.e., ZIF-8, ZIF-67, and MIL-53(Al)) in-situ etched during the addition with a metal phenolic network made of tannic acid and Fe (II) ions (Fig. 7) [87]. The coordi­ nation interactions between tannic acid and metal centers in MOFs guaranteed excellent compatibility between the metal phenolic network and MOFs. As a result, the methanol permeance of the modified mem­ branes increased to 24.7 LMH/bar, with an increment of $270\ \%$ compared with the control ones (9.1 LMH/bar), while the rejection of small organic molecules (i.e., Alcian blue) maintained higher than 99.0 $\%$ .
## 4.4. Forward osmosis (FO)
FO is a process in which water molecules traverse through a semipermeable membrane driven by the high osmotic pressure provided by a concentrated draw solution [155]. As no hydraulic pressure is applied in the separation process, the transport of water and solute molecules across the membrane is highly dependent on the intrinsic membrane structure and properties. The water flux, salt permeability, and reverse solute flux are typically evaluated under different testing modes; namely, (1) the active layer faces the feed solution (also referred to as the AL-FS or FO mode) and (2) the active layer faces the draw solution (i.e., the AL-DS or pressure retarded osmosis (PRO) mode) [156–160]. Usually, the selectivity of FO membranes is determined by the ratio of salt permeability to water permeance (i.e., B/A ratio). Therefore, a low B/A value is highly desirable in the FO process [161]. The antifouling propensity of membranes has also been intensively pursued and studied in FO processes [162].
Besides the enhanced antifouling property of TFN membranes doped with GO/Ag-MOF discussed in Section 3.3, they also showed a higher water permeability and selectivity than the TFC and TFN membranes comprising only Ag-MOFs [90]. The former had a water permeability coefficient of 2.45 LMH/bar and a B/A ratio: $0.09\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ , while the latter TFC membrane had values of 1.70 LMH/bar and $0.11~\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ , respec­ tively. The higher salt retention of the former was due to the stronger Donnan effects caused by the higher negativity. In another study, Dai et al. incorporated $0.03{-}0.12\ \mathrm{wt/v\%}$ of CuBDC nanosheets into the polyamide layer via interfacial polymerization [63]. The resultant TFN membranes had greater water fluxes of 22 and ${50}\mathrm{LMH}$ and considerably less reverse solute fluxes of 0.12 and $0.13\mathrm{g/L}$ under FO and PRO modes, respectively than the virgin TFC membranes (18/32 LMH and $0.25/0.27$ $^{\mathrm{g/L}}$ , respectively). The performance enhancements arose from the fact that (1) CuBDC had a pore aperture of $0.52\ \mathrm{nm}$ , larger than water molecules $(0.28\mathrm{nm})$ but sufficiently small to repel hydrated $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ (0.72 nm) and $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions $(0.66~\mathrm{nm})$ and (2) the presence of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions and –COOH groups on the MOF nanosheets resulted in a more hydrophilic membrane surface that mitigated the biofouling tendency as visualized via confocal laser scanning microscopy [63].
Qiu and He coated ZIF-8 nanoparticles by a thin hydrophilic layer of PDA and introduced them into the poly(ethylene imine) (PEI)/TMC polymerized matrix to (1) improve their dispersion in a PEI aqueous solution, and (2) enhance the affinity between ZIF-8 and the selective layer since PDA can not only strongly adhere to ZIF-8 but also react with TMC during the interfacial polymerization [163]. The optimal TFN membrane comprised a PD $\scriptstyle\lfloor{\mathcal{Q}}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{F}-8$ loading of $0.05\mathrm{wt\%}$ and possessed a higher water flux (20.8 vs 12.8 LMH) without losing much selectivity than the TFC membrane under the FO mode (i.e., Js/Jw ratio of 0.25 vs $0.20\textrm{g}/\mathrm{L})$ . By adopting a similar method, Eghbalazar and Shakeri embedded PDA coated UiO-66- $(\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}$ in the polyamide layer of FO membranes [51]. The optimized membrane consisted of $0.03\mathrm{wt\%}$ nanoparticles and exhibited a remarkably higher water flux $(22.2\mathrm{LMH})$ and better selectivity $\left(0.27~\mathrm{g/L}\right)$ than the bare TFC membrane (14.5 LMH and $0.36~\mathrm{g/L}\dot{}$ .
To ensure the integrity of MOFs in TFN membranes for heavy metal removal via FO processes, He et al. added PDA in the MPD aqueous solution first, and then synthesized the polyamide layer via interfacial polymerization between the aqueous phase and a TMC/MOF organic phase [164]. Because MOF-801 strongly adhered to PDA, it yielded a higher loading and a more uniform distribution of MOFs in the poly­ amide layer. Compared to the TFC membranes, the PDA/MOF TFN membranes showed a $30\%$ increment of water flux and a $44\%$ reduction of reverse salt flux. In addition, the modified membranes had high re­ jections $(>94~\%$ towards heavy metal ions (i.e., $2000~\mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{\bar{C}d^{2+}}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , and $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ ) due to the size exclusion effect and adsorption capa­ bility of MOFs.
## 4.5. Gas separation
Since MOFs have less instability issues in gases than in liquids, MOF incorporated TFN membranes have been extensively studied for gas separation [113,106,165,166], especially for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture to reduce the greenhouse effect. Also, hydrogen, as a clean energy source, has received unprecedented attention, and its demand is growing rapidly. However, the traditional methods to separate hydrogen such as pressure swing adsorption and cryogenic separation are energy intensive. Membranebased gas separation stands out in terms of low energy consumption, modest maintenance cost and effortless operation. Therefore, most MOF incorporated TFN membranes focus on the separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (i.e., $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture), $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (i.e., natural gas purification) and $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CO}}_{2}$ (i.e., hydrogen production) [165–167]. Improvements to gas separations by MOF-based TFN membranes include bolstering the membrane selec­ tivity by altering the pore structure of MOFs and long-term stability by relatively higher pore rigidity compared with polymer chains.
Li et al. proposed a swelling-controlled nanofiller positioning (SNP) method to pre-position ZIF-8 particles on a swollen PDMS intermediate layer in an organic solvent prior to interfacial polymerization as depic­ ted in Fig. 8a [168]. The swollen PDMS molecules could not only restrain ZIF-8 mobility but also enhance their mutual compatibility. Once a polyamide layer was formed on the modified support with prepositioned ZIF-8 nanoparticles, the gas transport was significantly improved. ZIF-8 nanoparticles with different diameters of about 40, 75 and $115\mathrm{nm}$ were prepared and added into the TFN membranes with a tuneable selective-layer thickness. It was found that the surface coverage of ZIF-8 decreased with an increase in particle diameter. The TFN-75 membrane had a $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeance of 2740 gas permeation unit (GPU) and a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 104. Since ZIF-8 had a pore aperture of $3.4\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ larger than the dynamic diameter of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}(3.3\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , the addition of ZIF-8 provided additional pathways for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ transport, which led to a higher $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeability and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity.
Although it is vital to develop polymeric membranes with a high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeability and selectivity, one must have a porous substrate with sufficient mechanical strength to support the thin selective layer. A porous substrate with large pore sizes is preferred to reduce the mass transport resistance, but it would risk the intrusion of polymer solutions into the porous substrate and reduce the overall permeance. Liu et al. in Prof. Qiao’s group incorporated MOF nanosheets into the gutter layer to strengthen the mechanical properties, minimize the intrusion and obtain a high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeability [106]. The MOF nanosheets were produced by means of a surfactant-assisted method, followed by coating them onto a porous substrate using vacuum filtration. Comparing with the conventional PDMS gutter layer, the smooth MOF nanosheets showed superior permeable performance. After spin-coating the PolyActive se­ lective layer onto the MOF gutter layer, the composite membrane exhibited impressive separation performance with a $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeance of around 2100 GPU and a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 30. As shown in Fig. 8b, the membranes also exhibited extraordinary flexibility which may be useful in gas separation operations.
Liu et al. also developed a TFC membrane comprising three layers: (1) a $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ selective layer made of polymer of intrinsic microporosity-1 (PIM-1) and MOFs; (2) a highly permeable PDMS gutter layer impreg­ nated with MOF nanosheets; and (3) a underneath polymeric porous substrate (Fig. 8c) [113]. The composite PDMS/MOF gutter layer had a $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeance of 10,000–11,000 GPU, suggesting it had minimal gas transport resistance. After blending MOF-74-Ni and $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ -UiO-66 into the membranes, the resultant TFN membrane had a $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeance increasing from 4320 to 4660–7460 GPU and a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity from 19 to 26–33 as compared with the pristine PIM-1. This was because there were strong interactions in MOF-74-Ni between the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , –OH groups of the MOF-74-Ni and PIM chains, leading to larger $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ selective regions and the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 also showed similar in­ teractions towards PIM chains. In addition, the PIM-1 $.@\mathrm{MOFs}$ based TFN membranes displayed anti-aging performance with a stable $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ per­ meance and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity because of strong interactions between polymer chains and MOF surface.
Mousavinejad and his colleagues employed nickel-based MOFs (Ni$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC) to fabricate $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ -philic Pebax TFN membranes [115]. The TFN membranes with a $5\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ MOF loading showed the best gas per­ formance as the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeability increased from 19.05 to 31.55 Barrer and the $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity jumped up from 32.2 to 94. The increased $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity of $\mathrm{\DeltaNi-NH_{2}}$ -BDC incorporated TFN membranes arose from two factors: (1) the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ group of $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ -BDC helped promote their compatibility with Pebax and reduce chain flexibility and (2) in­ teractions between the membranes and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ were enhanced due to both $\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ groups and metal sites in the MOF structures.
S´anchez-Laínez et al. prepared TFN membranes by incorporating ZIF-8 into polyamide-based TFN membranes via interfacial polymeri­ zation for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation [169]. Although the obtained TFN mem­ branes contained a low content of MOFs (i.e., $0.013\mathrm{g}\mathrm{m}^{-2}$ membrane), they showed a high $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CO}}_{2}$ selectivity up to 18.1 at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 6 bar. Besides, the membranes exhibited a stable performance at 180C for 7 days. Ma et al. in Prof. Meng’s group prepared thin 2D $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(b i m)_{4}}$ nanosheets on polyacrylonitrile (PAN) ultrafiltration membranes as a gutter layer by vacuum filtration and then fabricated the TFN mem­ branes via interfacial polymerization for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation [104]. The thin gutter layer allowed a high gas transport and thereby the TFN membrane had a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ permeance of 2447 GPU. Because $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ have kinetic diameters of 2.8 and $3.3\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , respectively, it was easier for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to permeate through the 2D MOF nanosheet than $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . Thus, the TFN membranes exhibited an outstanding $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity of 12.3–12.6 at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 5. Conclusions and outlook
A significant amount of experimental data has confirmed that MOF embedded TFN membranes can surpass the traditional TFC membranes in terms of separation performance and other properties due to the high selectivity and permeability as well as the adjustable pore structure and surface chemistry of MOFs. Future R&D should focus on (1) molecular design of novel MOFs with special functionality, (2) molecular model­ ling of transport and separation mechanisms across MOF TFN mem­ branes, (3) streamline the current fabrication technologies for MOF based TFN membranes and (4) scale up the manufacture process and commercialization.
Although new MOF nanomaterials may continuously spring up, more attempts should be focused on the interfacial chemistry to seamlessly integrate MOFs with polymeric matrices during the construction of TFN membranes. (1) Magnetically responsive MOFs might be attractive, so one may position them at the exact places in the selective layer using magnetic field assisted protocols to design TFN membranes with desir­ able performance [176]. (2) In addition, combinations between MOFs and other nanomaterials (i.e., MOF composite or hybrid materials or multi-functional MOFs) should be explored. Currently, only MOFs in­ tegrated with carbon-based materials (i.e., GO) have been studied. However, taking advantages of both materials, one may envisage that MOF composite/hybrids can impart TFN membranes with useful func­ tionalities such as photocatalysis [177–179].
Molecular modelling and simulation should be employed because they have played a vital role to advance the fundamental understanding of membrane materials, design and formation, and separation mecha­ nisms [180,181]. The synergetic combination of molecular simulation and advanced experiments may provide additional insights to reveal the inherent relationships among MOF’s chemistry and pore structure, polymer matrix, processing parameters, membrane structure and sepa­ ration performance. Thus, the next generation TFN membranes with precise separations for chemical, environmental, pharmaceutical and petrochemical industries can be developed.
In addition, most MOF TFN membranes developed to date are still at the research stage, while the technology to produce TFC membranes in large scale is commercially available. In order to successfully adopt the lab-scale fabrication technologies in the existing manufacture process of TFC membranes, several factors must be considered and investigated. (1) Firstly, the current fabrication technologies for MOF based TFN membranes must be scrutinized and simplified to lower the fabrication steps and increase the productivity. In other words, simple and low-cost blending methods should be developed, which can be fitted into the current manufacturing process for TFC membranes and ultimately establish a controllable, scalable, and cost-effective fabrication process for MOF TFN membranes. (2) Secondly, the economic benefits of using MOF TFN membranes must outweigh the additional costs in develop­ ment and production. (3) Moreover, most studies on MOF TFN membranes were currently evaluated using highly idealized feed solu­ tions for a short duration, while it is vital to use real industrial or municipal wastewater or flue gas under long-term evaluation protocols. From an industrial perspective, one must use real waste to test a po­ tential membrane product before considering its scaling up and commercialization.
